he shortest way. Lives in the Sunder region. Has been seen in the same city on two turns running. Between 46 and 55 years old. Name starts with S. Return their id and nothing else.",why$i="A Handler file wants the shapes rather than the syntax: a distance that has to be worked out, a trail that has to be read in order. The tables are the same ones you started on.",world$i="handler",seed$i="academy-again-handler",review$3=!0,check$i={kind:"rows",sql:"SELECT p.id FROM people p WHERE p.occupation = 'baker' AND p.city_id IN ( WITH RECURSIVE hop(a, b) AS (SELECT city_a, city_b FROM roads UNION SELECT city_b, city_a FROM roads), reach(id, hops) AS ( SELECT id, 0 FROM cities WHERE name = 'Lorne' UNION SELECT hop.b, reach.hops + 1 FROM reach JOIN hop ON hop.a = reach.id WHERE reach.hops < 2 ) SELECT id FROM reach GROUP BY id HAVING min(hops) = 2 ) AND p.city_id IN (SELECT id FROM cities WHERE region_id = (SELECT id FROM regions WHERE name = 'Sunder')) AND p.id IN ( SELECT person_id FROM (SELECT person_id, city_id, turn, lag(city_id) OVER trail AS city_before, lag(turn) OVER trail AS turn_before FROM sightings WINDOW trail AS (PARTITION BY person_id ORDER BY turn)) WHERE city_id = city_before AND turn = turn_before + 1 ) AND p.age BETWEEN 46 AND 55 AND p.name LIKE 'S%'"},hints$i=["Start with the cheap clues: the trade, the letter, the age, the region. See how many that leaves before writing anything recursive.","The distance clue is a list of city ids, so it goes in as p.city_id IN (...). The run clue is a list of person ids, so it goes in as p.id IN (...).","If the distance list looks too long, check whether the city you started from is in it."],solution$i="SELECT p.id FROM people p WHERE p.occupation = 'baker' AND p.city_id IN ( WITH RECURSIVE hop(a, b) AS (SELECT city_a, city_b FROM roads UNION SELECT city_b, city_a FROM roads), reach(id, hops) AS ( SELECT id, 0 FROM cities WHERE name = 'Lorne' UNION SELECT hop.b, reach.hops + 1 FROM reach JOIN hop ON hop.a = reach.id WHERE reach.hops < 2 ) SELECT id FROM reach GROUP BY id HAVING min(hops) = 2 ) AND p.city_id IN (SELECT id FROM cities WHERE region_id = (SELECT id FROM regions WHERE name = 'Sunder')) AND p.id IN ( SELECT person_id FROM (SELECT person_id, city_id, turn, lag(city_id) OVER trail AS city_before, lag(turn) OVER trail AS turn_before FROM sightings WINDOW trail AS (PARTITION BY person_id ORDER BY turn)) WHERE city_id = city_before AND turn = turn_before + 1 ) AND p.age BETWEEN 46 AND 55 AND p.name LIKE 'S%'",__vite_glob_0_44={id:id$i,mode:mode$i,title:title$i,brief:brief$i,task:task$i,why:why$i,world:world$i,seed:seed$i,review:review$3,check:check$i,hints:hints$i,solution:solution$i},id$h="seek-index-first",mode$h="seek",stage$h="Indexes",allow$6="index",title$h="A way in",brief$h="Every lesson so far took the tables as they came. In this stage you may change how they are stored. An index is a sorted copy of one column, with a note beside each value of the row it came from. SQLite can go straight to a value in a sorted list. Without one it reads every row, and the plan says SCAN.",task$h="The query in the console finds everyone aged 43, and reads all of people to do it. Give people an index on age, then run the query again. It should scan no rows at all.",why$h="An index is a second way in to the same rows, kept in order so the engine can go straight to the ones it wants. The table is unchanged; what changes is the route to it.",world$h="handler",seed$h="academy-index",starter$b="SELECT id, name FROM people WHERE age = 43",check$h={kind:"rows",sql:"SELECT id, name FROM people WHERE age = 43",maxScan:0,planHas:["USING INDEX"]},hints$h=["Run the query as it stands and read the line under the result: rows scanned, and a plan that says SCAN people.","CREATE INDEX wants a name for the index, then the table, then the column in brackets. Run it on its own. It returns nothing.","CREATE INDEX people_age ON people(age); then the same SELECT again. The plan now says SEARCH people USING INDEX people_age."],solution$h="CREATE INDEX people_age ON people(age); SELECT id, name FROM people WHERE age = 43",__vite_glob_0_45={id:id$h,mode:mode$h,stage:stage$h,allow:allow$6,title:title$h,brief:brief$h,task:task$h,why:why$h,world:world$h,seed:seed$h,starter:starter$b,check:check$h,hints:hints$h,solution:solution$h},id$g="seek-index-foreign-key",mode$g="seek",stage$g="Indexes",allow$5="index",title$g="The key nobody indexed",brief$g="A primary key gets an index without being asked, which is why a search by id has never scanned anything. A foreign key gets none. sightings.person_id points at people.id, every question about one person's trail goes through it, and the only way into sightings is from the top.",task$g="The query in the console asks where person 251 was seen. It reads every sighting on file to find five. Index the column it searches on, and run it again without scanning a row.",why$g="SQLite indexes a primary key for you. It does not index the columns pointing at it, and those are the ones joins land on, which is why this is the index most often missing.",world$g="handler",seed$g="academy-index",starter$a="SELECT city_id, turn FROM sightings WHERE person_id = 251",check$g={kind:"rows",sql:"SELECT city_id, turn FROM sightings WHERE person_id = 251",maxScan:0,planHas:["USING INDEX"]},hints$g=["The column to index is the one in the WHERE, not the ones in the SELECT.","The index goes on sightings, and the column is person_id.","CREATE INDEX sightings_person ON sightings(person_id); then the same SELECT again."],solution$g="CREATE INDEX sightings_person ON sightings(person_id); SELECT city_id, turn FROM sightings WHERE person_id = 251",__vite_glob_0_46={id:id$g,mode:mode$g,stage:stage$g,allow:allow$5,title:title$g,brief:brief$g,task:task$g,why:why$g,world:world$g,seed:seed$g,starter:starter$a,check:check$g,hints:hints$g,solution:solution$g},id$f="seek-index-order",mode$f="seek",stage$f="Indexes",allow$4="index",setup$6="CREATE INDEX people_age ON people(age)",title$f="Two columns, one order",brief$f="An index can hold more than one column, and the order decides what it is good for. It is sorted by the first column, and by the second only within one value of the first, the way a phone book runs by surname and then by first name. Put the column tested with = first and the one tested with a range second, and the rows wanted sit together in one run.",task$f="Smiths over 50. people has an index on age, so the plan finds everyone over 50 and then looks at each one's trade. Make one index that answers both tests. The plan should read occupation=? AND age>?.",why$f="An index is sorted by its first column, then within that by its second. Put the column you match exactly first and the one you range over second, or the second half of the index is decoration.",world$f="handler",seed$f="academy-index",starter$9="SELECT id, name FROM people WHERE occupation = 'smith' AND age > 50",check$f={kind:"rows",sql:"SELECT id, name FROM people WHERE occupation = 'smith' AND age > 50",planHas:["occupation=? AND age>?"]},hints$f=["Run it and read the plan. It names people_age and only one of the two tests: (age>?). The other test is done row by row afterwards.","An index on (age, occupation) does no better. After a range on the first column the second is no longer in order, so SQLite cannot use it.","CREATE INDEX people_trade_age ON people(occupation, age); then the same SELECT again."],solution$f="CREATE INDEX people_trade_age ON people(occupation, age); SELECT id, name FROM people WHERE occupation = 'smith' AND age > 50",__vite_glob_0_47={id:id$f,mode:mode$f,stage:stage$f,allow:allow$4,setup:setup$6,title:title$f,brief:brief$f,task:task$f,why:why$f,world:world$f,seed:seed$f,starter:starter$9,check:check$f,hints:hints$f,solution:solution$f},id$e="seek-index-covering",mode$e="seek",stage$e="Indexes",allow$3="index",setup$5="CREATE INDEX people_trade ON people(occupation)",title$e="Never opening the table",brief$e="An index entry holds the indexed columns and the address of the row. Finding the entry is one step and fetching the row is another. If the index already holds every column the query asks for, the second step never happens. The plan calls that a COVERING INDEX.",task$e="The names of the masons. The index on occupation finds them, and then each row is fetched for its name. Make an index the whole query can be answered from, so the plan says COVERING INDEX.",why$e="When an index holds every column a query asks for, the table is never opened at all. The plan says COVERING INDEX, and the rows it no longer reads are the ones you were paying for.",world$e="handler",seed$e="academy-index",starter$8="SELECT name FROM people WHERE occupation = 'mason'",check$e={kind:"rows",sql:"SELECT name FROM people WHERE occupation = 'mason'",planHas:["COVERING INDEX"]},hints$e=["The query touches two columns: occupation to find the rows, name to answer with.","Both go in one index. The one being searched on comes first.","CREATE INDEX people_trade_name ON people(occupation, name); then the same SELECT again."],solution$e="CREATE INDEX people_trade_name ON people(occupation, name); SELECT name FROM people WHERE occupation = 'mason'",__vite_glob_0_48={id:id$e,mode:mode$e,stage:stage$e,allow:allow$3,setup:setup$5,title:title$e,brief:brief$e,task:task$e,why:why$e,world:world$e,seed:seed$e,starter:starter$8,check:check$e,hints:hints$e,solution:solution$e},id$d="seek-index-unused",mode$d="seek",stage$d="Indexes",allow$2="index",setup$4="CREATE INDEX people_age ON people(age)",title$d="An index left on the shelf",brief$d="An index is sorted by what is in the column. Wrap the column in arithmetic or a function and the query is asking about a value the index is not sorted by, so SQLite goes back to reading every row. The index is still there. The query has stopped it being used.",task$d="people has an index on age, and the query in the console scans every row anyway. Ask for the same people in a way that lets the index work: no rows scanned.",why$d="Wrap a column in a function and you have hidden it from its own index. The engine indexed the column, not the expression, so ask about the column.",world$d="handler",seed$d="academy-index",starter$7="SELECT id, name FROM people WHERE age + 1 = 44",check$d={kind:"rows",sql:"SELECT id, name FROM people WHERE age = 43",maxScan:0,planHas:["USING INDEX"]},hints$d=["Run it and read the plan: SCAN people, though people_age exists.","Leave the column alone on its side of the = and do the arithmetic on the other side, in your head.","SELECT id, name FROM people WHERE age = 43"],solution$d="SELECT id, name FROM people WHERE age = 43",__vite_glob_0_49={id:id$d,mode:mode$d,stage:stage$d,allow:allow$2,setup:setup$4,title:title$d,brief:brief$d,task:task$d,why:why$d,world:world$d,seed:seed$d,starter:starter$7,check:check$d,hints:hints$d,solution:solution$d},id$c="seek-index-sort",mode$c="seek",stage$c="Indexes",allow$1="index",title$c="Already in order",brief$c="Sorting is work as well. ORDER BY on a column with no index means reading every row and sorting the lot, even when LIMIT only wants five of them. The plan says USE TEMP B-TREE FOR ORDER BY. An index is in order already, so SQLite can walk it from one end and stop when it has enough.",task$c="The five oldest people, newest id first among those of the same age. The query in the console reads and sorts everyone to find five. Make the sort disappear from the plan, and scan 10 rows or fewer.",why$c="An index is already in order, so the right one turns a sort into a walk. The plan stops mentioning a temporary b-tree, and that was the part costing you.",world$c="handler",seed$c="academy-index",starter$6="SELECT name, age FROM people ORDER BY age DESC, id DESC LIMIT 5",check$c={kind:"rows",sql:"SELECT name, age FROM people ORDER BY age DESC, id DESC LIMIT 5",ordered:!0,maxScan:10,planLacks:["TEMP B-TREE"]},hints$c=["The query does not need to change. What is missing is something already sorted by age.","Every index ends with the row's id, so an index on age is also in id order within each age. It can be read backwards as well as forwards.","CREATE INDEX people_age ON people(age); then the same SELECT again. The plan says SCAN people USING INDEX people_age, and it stops after five."],solution$c="CREATE INDEX people_age ON people(age); SELECT name, age FROM people ORDER BY age DESC, id DESC LIMIT 5",__vite_glob_0_50={id:id$c,mode:mode$c,stage:stage$c,allow:allow$1,title:title$c,brief:brief$c,task:task$c,why:why$c,world:world$c,seed:seed$c,starter:starter$6,check:check$c,hints:hints$c,solution:solution$c},id$b="seek-again-index",mode$b="seek",stage$b="Indexes",allow="index",review$2=!0,title$b="Indexes again",brief$b="A different world, and nothing in it is indexed but the ids. Read the plan, decide which table is being read from the top, and decide what order its index has to be in.",task$b="Who was seen in city 7 from turn 6 onwards, and when. The query in the console is right and scans every sighting. Run it with one index doing both tests, so the plan reads city_id=? AND turn>?.",why$b="Read the plan first and index second. Anyone can add indexes until something improves; the plan tells you which one was going to.",world$b="handler",seed$b="academy-index-again",starter$5="SELECT p.name, s.turn FROM sightings s JOIN people p ON p.id = s.person_id WHERE s.city_id = 7 AND s.turn >= 6",check$b={kind:"rows",sql:"SELECT p.name, s.turn FROM sightings s JOIN people p ON p.id = s.person_id WHERE s.city_id = 7 AND s.turn >= 6",maxScan:0,planHas:["city_id=? AND turn>?"]},hints$b=["The plan has two lines. One of them is already a SEARCH, by primary key. The other is the one to fix.","Two tests on sightings: one with =, one with a range. The order of the columns in the index follows from that."],solution$b="CREATE INDEX sightings_city_turn ON sightings(city_id, turn); SELECT p.name, s.turn FROM sightings s JOIN people p ON p.id = s.person_id WHERE s.city_id = 7 AND s.turn >= 6",__vite_glob_0_51={id:id$b,mode:mode$b,stage:stage$b,allow,review:review$2,title:title$b,brief:brief$b,task:task$b,why:why$b,world:world$b,seed:seed$b,starter:starter$5,check:check$b,hints:hints$b,solution:solution$b},mode$a="seek",world$a="handler",seed$a="academy-postgres",engine$a="postgres",stage$a="PostgreSQL",id$a="pg-strict",title$a="Another engine",brief$a="Everything so far ran on SQLite. This stage runs on PostgreSQL, the database most jobs mean when they say SQL. The same world is loaded into it and nearly every query you have written works unchanged. What changes is how strict it is: a column has a type, and Postgres refuses to guess when the types do not fit, where SQLite would quietly pick something. Try SELECT 'age ' + age FROM people and read what comes back.",task$a="Person 119, with a label: their name, and the text age 36 made by joining 'age ' to their age with ||, which is how text is joined.",check$a={kind:"rows",sql:"SELECT name, 'age ' || age AS label FROM people WHERE id = 119"},hints$a=["Run the query from the brief first. Postgres tried to read the text 'age ' as an integer so it could add it, and could not. On SQLite the same query returns 36.","|| joins text, and Postgres will turn a number into text to do it.","SELECT name, 'age ' || age AS label FROM people WHERE id = 119"],solution$a="SELECT name, 'age ' || age AS label FROM people WHERE id = 119",why$a="SQLite would have run the original and returned 36, because it adds whatever it is given. Postgres stopped and said where and why. Strictness is a cost when you are typing and a gift when a mistake would otherwise hide in a result.",__vite_glob_0_52={mode:mode$a,world:world$a,seed:seed$a,engine:engine$a,stage:stage$a,id:id$a,title:title$a,brief:brief$a,task:task$a,check:check$a,hints:hints$a,solution:solution$a,why:why$a},mode$9="seek",world$9="handler",seed$9="academy-postgres",engine$9="postgres",stage$9="PostgreSQL",id$9="pg-ilike",title$9="Case counts now",brief$9="In SQLite, LIKE ignores the case of letters. In Postgres it does not: LIKE 'a%' finds names that start with a small a, and nobody's name does. Postgres has a second operator, ILIKE, that matches the way SQLite's LIKE did.",task$9="The query in the console finds nobody. Return the names of everyone whose name begins with A, in either case.",starter$4="SELECT name FROM people WHERE name LIKE 'a%'",check$9={kind:"rows",sql:"SELECT name FROM people WHERE name ILIKE 'a%'"},hints$9=["Nothing is wrong with the pattern. The letter is.","ILIKE is LIKE with the case ignored. A capital A in the pattern would do here too, and would miss anyone whose name was filed in lower case.","SELECT name FROM people WHERE name ILIKE 'a%'"],solution$9="SELECT name FROM people WHERE name ILIKE 'a%'",why$9="The same query gave 13 rows on SQLite and none here, with no error. Differences between engines mostly look like this: not a refusal, but a different answer. ILIKE is the Postgres spelling; on SQLite it does not exist.",__vite_glob_0_53={mode:mode$9,world:world$9,seed:seed$9,engine:engine$9,stage:stage$9,id:id$9,title:title$9,brief:brief$9,task:task$9,starter:starter$4,check:check$9,hints:hints$9,solution:solution$9,why:why$9},mode$8="seek",world$8="handler",seed$8="academy-postgres",engine$8="postgres",stage$8="PostgreSQL",id$8="pg-distinct-on",title$8="One row per city",brief$8="The oldest person in each city needs a row per city, and GROUP BY can only give max(age), not the name that goes with it. Postgres has DISTINCT ON: sort the rows so the one you want comes first in each group, then keep the first of each. The columns in DISTINCT ON have to lead the ORDER BY.",task$8="The oldest person in each city: city_id, name and age. Where two share the top age, the lower id wins.",check$8={kind:"rows",sql:"SELECT DISTINCT ON (city_id) city_id, name, age FROM people ORDER BY city_id, age DESC, id"},hints$8=["Sort by city first, then by what makes a row the one to keep.","DISTINCT ON (city_id) keeps the first row of each city as sorted. ORDER BY city_id, age DESC, id puts the oldest first, and the lower id first among equals.","SELECT DISTINCT ON (city_id) city_id, name, age FROM people ORDER BY city_id, age DESC, id"],solution$8="SELECT DISTINCT ON (city_id) city_id, name, age FROM people ORDER BY city_id, age DESC, id",why$8="On SQLite this takes a window function and a subquery, or a correlated subquery that reads people once per city. DISTINCT ON is one sort and one pass. It is Postgres only, which is the trade: less to write, and a query that will not travel.",__vite_glob_0_54={mode:mode$8,world:world$8,seed:seed$8,engine:engine$8,stage:stage$8,id:id$8,title:title$8,brief:brief$8,task:task$8,check:check$8,hints:hints$8,solution:solution$8,why:why$8},mode$7="seek",world$7="handler",seed$7="academy-postgres",engine$7="postgres",stage$7="PostgreSQL",id$7="pg-filter",title$7="Counting with a condition",brief$7="Counting the rows that meet a condition inside a group is usually sum(CASE WHEN ... THEN 1 ELSE 0 END). Postgres lets an aggregate carry its own WHERE: count(*) FILTER (WHERE age > 50). It reads as what it is, and it works on any aggregate.",task$7="For each occupation, how many people have it and how many of those are over 50. Three columns: occupation, the count, the count over 50.",check$7={kind:"rows",sql:"SELECT occupation, count(*), count(*) FILTER (WHERE age > 50) FROM people GROUP BY occupation"},hints$7=["One GROUP BY, two counts. The second count only sees some of the rows.","FILTER (WHERE age > 50) goes straight after count(*).","SELECT occupation, count(*), count(*) FILTER (WHERE age > 50) FROM people GROUP BY occupation"],solution$7="SELECT occupation, count(*), count(*) FILTER (WHERE age > 50) FROM people GROUP BY occupation",why$7="sum(CASE ...) passes this lesson too, and it is what to write on an engine without FILTER. The difference shows on avg and max: a CASE that returns 0 for the rows you do not want drags an average down, where FILTER leaves them out.",__vite_glob_0_55={mode:mode$7,world:world$7,seed:seed$7,engine:engine$7,stage:stage$7,id:id$7,title:title$7,brief:brief$7,task:task$7,check:check$7,hints:hints$7,solution:solution$7,why:why$7},mode$6="seek",world$6="handler",seed$6="academy-postgres",engine$6="postgres",stage$6="PostgreSQL",id$6="pg-string-agg",title$6="Many rows, one line",brief$6="An aggregate does not have to produce a number. string_agg joins the values of a group into one piece of text, with a separator of your choosing, and takes an ORDER BY of its own so the order inside the text is not left to chance. SQLite's group_concat is the same idea with less control.",task$6="The names of every smith, in one row and one column, separated by a comma and a space, in alphabetical order.",check$6={kind:"rows",sql:"SELECT string_agg(name, ', ' ORDER BY name) FROM people WHERE occupation = 'smith'"},hints$6=["An aggregate over the smiths, with no GROUP BY, gives one row.","string_agg(name, ', ' ORDER BY name). The ORDER BY sits inside the brackets.","SELECT string_agg(name, ', ' ORDER BY name) FROM people WHERE occupation = 'smith'"],solution$6="SELECT string_agg(name, ', ' ORDER BY name) FROM people WHERE occupation = 'smith'",why$6="A report wants a list in a cell more often than you would think. The ORDER BY inside the aggregate is the part people miss: without it the names come in whatever order the rows arrived, which differs from run to run.",__vite_glob_0_56={mode:mode$6,world:world$6,seed:seed$6,engine:engine$6,stage:stage$6,id:id$6,title:title$6,brief:brief$6,task:task$6,check:check$6,hints:hints$6,solution:solution$6,why:why$6},mode$5="seek",world$5="handler",seed$5="academy-postgres",engine$5="postgres",stage$5="PostgreSQL",id$5="pg-generate-series",title$5="Rows from nothing",brief$5="GROUP BY can only group what is there. Ask how many roads fall in each 50-mile band of distance and a band with no road in it never appears. generate_series makes rows out of a range, and a LEFT JOIN from those rows to the roads keeps every band, empty ones included.",task$5="Roads by distance band: every band from 0 to 400 in steps of 50, and the number of roads whose distance is at least the band and less than the next one. Bands with no road show 0.",check$5={kind:"rows",sql:"SELECT band, count(r.distance) FROM generate_series(0, 400, 50) AS band LEFT JOIN roads r ON r.distance >= band AND r.distance < band + 50 GROUP BY band"},hints$5=["generate_series(0, 400, 50) is a table with one column and nine rows.","LEFT JOIN roads to it on the distance being inside the band. count(r.distance) counts matches; count(*) would count the empty band as 1.","SELECT band, count(r.distance) FROM generate_series(0, 400, 50) AS band LEFT JOIN roads r ON r.distance >= band AND r.distance < band + 50 GROUP BY band"],solution$5="SELECT band, count(r.distance) FROM generate_series(0, 400, 50) AS band LEFT JOIN roads r ON r.distance >= band AND r.distance < band + 50 GROUP BY band",why$5="Charts with a missing bar, reports with a month that vanished because nothing happened in it: the fix is always the same, start from the full set of rows you want and join the data onto it. On SQLite a recursive CTE makes the series. Here it is a function.",__vite_glob_0_57={mode:mode$5,world:world$5,seed:seed$5,engine:engine$5,stage:stage$5,id:id$5,title:title$5,brief:brief$5,task:task$5,check:check$5,hints:hints$5,solution:solution$5,why:why$5},mode$4="seek",world$4="handler",seed$4="academy-postgres",engine$4="postgres",stage$4="PostgreSQL",id$4="pg-lateral",title$4="A subquery that can see out",brief$4="A subquery in FROM normally cannot refer to the tables beside it. LATERAL lets it: the subquery runs once for each row of the table to its left and can use that row's columns. For each city, the latest sighting there is one ORDER BY and LIMIT 1 inside a LATERAL, instead of a window function over all of sightings.",task$4="For every city, its latest sighting: the city's name, the turn, and the person_id. Latest means the highest turn, and the highest sighting id among sightings on the same turn.",check$4={kind:"rows",sql:"SELECT c.name, s.turn, s.person_id FROM cities c, LATERAL (SELECT turn, person_id FROM sightings WHERE city_id = c.id ORDER BY turn DESC, id DESC LIMIT 1) s"},hints$4=["FROM cities c, LATERAL (...) s. Inside the brackets, c.id is in reach.","The subquery is the whole question for one city: WHERE city_id = c.id ORDER BY turn DESC, id DESC LIMIT 1.","SELECT c.name, s.turn, s.person_id FROM cities c, LATERAL (SELECT turn, person_id FROM sightings WHERE city_id = c.id ORDER BY turn DESC, id DESC LIMIT 1) s"],solution$4="SELECT c.name, s.turn, s.person_id FROM cities c, LATERAL (SELECT turn, person_id FROM sightings WHERE city_id = c.id ORDER BY turn DESC, id DESC LIMIT 1) s",why$4="The plan is worth a look here. The subquery ran 20 times, once per city, and each run read all of sightings: a top-N sort over 892 rows, twenty times. LATERAL is clear to read and it is a loop. With an index on sightings(city_id, turn) each run would be a short walk instead.",__vite_glob_0_58={mode:mode$4,world:world$4,seed:seed$4,engine:engine$4,stage:stage$4,id:id$4,title:title$4,brief:brief$4,task:task$4,check:check$4,hints:hints$4,solution:solution$4,why:why$4},mode$3="seek",world$3="handler",seed$3="academy-postgres",engine$3="postgres",stage$3="PostgreSQL",id$3="pg-explain",setup$3="CREATE TABLE archive AS SELECT g::integer AS id, (1 + (g * 7919) % 255)::integer AS person_id, (1 + (g * 104729) % 20)::integer AS city_id, (1 + (g * 31) % 400)::integer AS turn FROM generate_series(1::bigint, 40000) g; ALTER TABLE archive ADD PRIMARY KEY (id); ANALYZE archive",title$3="Reading the plan",brief$3="This world has a table SQLite never had: archive, 40,000 old sightings. Postgres's plan is longer than SQLite's and it is worth every line. Seq Scan means the whole table was read. cost is the planner's guess, actual time and rows are what happened, and Rows Removed by Filter is how many rows were read and thrown away.",task$3="The query in the console finds person 7 in the archive and reads all 40,000 rows to do it. Index the column it filters on and run it again. The plan should name the index, and no rows should be scanned.",starter$3="SELECT turn FROM archive WHERE person_id = 7",check$3={kind:"rows",sql:"SELECT turn FROM archive WHERE person_id = 7",maxScan:0,planHas:["Index"]},hints$3=["Run it and read the plan: Seq Scan on archive, and Rows Removed by Filter: 39843. Then look at Execution Time.","CREATE INDEX archive_person ON archive(person_id), the same as on SQLite. Run the query again and compare the two plans.","CREATE INDEX archive_person ON archive(person_id); then the same SELECT again. The plan reads Bitmap Index Scan on archive_person, and the execution time drops by more than ten times."],solution$3="CREATE INDEX archive_person ON archive(person_id); SELECT turn FROM archive WHERE person_id = 7",why$3="Two numbers in a Postgres plan are worth reading first: rows= in the estimate against rows= in the actual, and Rows Removed by Filter. When the estimate is far from the actual, the planner is working from stale statistics and ANALYZE fixes it. When Rows Removed is large, an index is missing. This plan had both right and one missing.",__vite_glob_0_59={mode:mode$3,world:world$3,seed:seed$3,engine:engine$3,stage:stage$3,id:id$3,setup:setup$3,title:title$3,brief:brief$3,task:task$3,starter:starter$3,check:check$3,hints:hints$3,solution:solution$3,why:why$3},mode$2="seek",world$2="handler",seed$2="academy-postgres",engine$2="postgres",stage$2="PostgreSQL",id$2="pg-composite",setup$2="CREATE TABLE archive AS SELECT g::integer AS id, (1 + (g * 7919) % 255)::integer AS person_id, (1 + (g * 104729) % 20)::integer AS city_id, (1 + (g * 31) % 400)::integer AS turn FROM generate_series(1::bigint, 40000) g; ALTER TABLE archive ADD PRIMARY KEY (id); ANALYZE archive; CREATE INDEX archive_person ON archive(person_id)",title$2="Two conditions, one index",brief$2="archive has an index on person_id. The query in the console asks for one person on the late turns, and the plan shows the index finding the person and then a Filter throwing most of those rows away: Rows Removed by Filter: 117. An index over both columns, the equality first, lets the index answer both conditions and hand back only the rows wanted.",task$2="Make an index that answers both conditions. The plan should say Index Cond: ((person_id = 7) AND (turn > 300)), with no Filter left.",starter$2="SELECT turn FROM archive WHERE person_id = 7 AND turn > 300",check$2={kind:"rows",sql:"SELECT turn FROM archive WHERE person_id = 7 AND turn > 300",planHas:["Index Cond: ((person_id = 7) AND (turn > 300))"],planLacks:["Rows Removed by Filter"]},hints$2=["Run it and find the two lines: Index Cond, which the index answered, and Filter, which it could not.","The order is the same rule as on SQLite: the column tested with = first, the range second.","CREATE INDEX archive_person_turn ON archive(person_id, turn); then the same SELECT again."],solution$2="CREATE INDEX archive_person_turn ON archive(person_id, turn); SELECT turn FROM archive WHERE person_id = 7 AND turn > 300",why$2="Index Cond and Filter are the two lines that tell you how much of the work the index did. Anything under Filter was fetched from the table and then discarded. The rule for column order does not change between engines; only the words in the plan do.",__vite_glob_0_60={mode:mode$2,world:world$2,seed:seed$2,engine:engine$2,stage:stage$2,id:id$2,setup:setup$2,title:title$2,brief:brief$2,task:task$2,starter:starter$2,check:check$2,hints:hints$2,solution:solution$2,why:why$2},mode$1="seek",world$1="handler",seed$1="academy-postgres",engine$1="postgres",stage$1="PostgreSQL",id$1="pg-index-only",setup$1="CREATE TABLE archive AS SELECT g::integer AS id, (1 + (g * 7919) % 255)::integer AS person_id, (1 + (g * 104729) % 20)::integer AS city_id, (1 + (g * 31) % 400)::integer AS turn FROM generate_series(1::bigint, 40000) g; ALTER TABLE archive ADD PRIMARY KEY (id); ANALYZE archive; CREATE INDEX archive_person ON archive(person_id); VACUUM archive",title$1="Never touching the table",brief$1="An index scan finds entries in the index and then fetches each row from the table for the columns it needs. If the index already holds every column the query asks for, the fetch is skipped and the plan says Index Only Scan. This is Postgres's name for what SQLite called a covering index, and Heap Fetches: 0 is the proof.",task$1="The query wants only turn for person 7. archive is indexed on person_id alone, so every match is fetched from the table. Make an index the query can be answered from, so the plan says Index Only Scan.",starter$1="SELECT turn FROM archive WHERE person_id = 7",check$1={kind:"rows",sql:"SELECT turn FROM archive WHERE person_id = 7",planHas:["Index Only Scan"]},hints$1=["The query touches two columns: person_id to find the rows, turn to answer with.","Both go in one index, the searched column first.","CREATE INDEX archive_person_turn ON archive(person_id, turn); then the same SELECT again."],solution$1="CREATE INDEX archive_person_turn ON archive(person_id, turn); SELECT turn FROM archive WHERE person_id = 7",why$1="Heap Fetches is the number of times an index-only scan had to look at the table after all, to check that a row is still current. On a freshly vacuumed table it is 0; on a busy table it creeps up until the next vacuum. It is one of the few plan lines that depends on the table's history and not the query.",__vite_glob_0_61={mode:mode$1,world:world$1,seed:seed$1,engine:engine$1,stage:stage$1,id:id$1,setup:setup$1,title:title$1,brief:brief$1,task:task$1,starter:starter$1,check:check$1,hints:hints$1,solution:solution$1,why:why$1},mode="seek",world="handler",seed="academy-postgres",engine="postgres",stage="PostgreSQL",id="pg-again",setup="CREATE TABLE archive AS SELECT g::integer AS id, (1 + (g * 7919) % 255)::integer AS 