s.city_id <> p.city_id THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) FROM people p JOIN sightings s ON s.person_id = p.id WHERE p.city_id = (SELECT id FROM cities WHERE name = 'Tamsin') GROUP BY p.id"],solution$o="SELECT p.id, sum(CASE WHEN s.city_id = p.city_id THEN 1 ELSE 0 END), sum(CASE WHEN s.city_id <> p.city_id THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) FROM people p JOIN sightings s ON s.person_id = p.id WHERE p.city_id = (SELECT id FROM cities WHERE name = 'Tamsin') GROUP BY p.id",__vite_glob_0_38={id:id$o,mode:mode$o,title:title$o,brief:brief$o,task:task$o,why:why$o,world:world$o,seed:seed$o,check:check$o,hints:hints$o,solution:solution$o},id$n="seek-cte",mode$n="seek",title$n="Naming a step",brief$n="WITH gives a query a name for as long as the statement lasts. It is a view that cleans up after itself. The point is not tidiness: once a step has a name you can use it twice, and some questions need that.",task$n="Return the name of every city with more sightings than the average city has. Count the sightings per city once, and use that count twice.",why$n="Naming a step makes a query readable and computes it once. A WITH block is a table that exists for the length of one statement.",world$n="handler",seed$n="academy-cte",check$n={kind:"rows",sql:"WITH seen AS (SELECT city_id, count(*) AS times FROM sightings GROUP BY city_id) SELECT c.name FROM seen JOIN cities c ON c.id = seen.city_id WHERE seen.times > (SELECT avg(times) FROM seen)"},hints$n=["WITH seen AS (SELECT city_id, count(*) AS times FROM sightings GROUP BY city_id) and then a SELECT that treats seen as a table.","The average is (SELECT avg(times) FROM seen). The filter is seen.times greater than that.","WITH seen AS (SELECT city_id, count(*) AS times FROM sightings GROUP BY city_id) SELECT c.name FROM seen JOIN cities c ON c.id = seen.city_id WHERE seen.times > (SELECT avg(times) FROM seen)"],solution$n="WITH seen AS (SELECT city_id, count(*) AS times FROM sightings GROUP BY city_id) SELECT c.name FROM seen JOIN cities c ON c.id = seen.city_id WHERE seen.times > (SELECT avg(times) FROM seen)",__vite_glob_0_39={id:id$n,mode:mode$n,title:title$n,brief:brief$n,task:task$n,why:why$n,world:world$n,seed:seed$n,check:check$n,hints:hints$n,solution:solution$n},id$m="seek-window",mode$m="seek",title$m="The latest of each",brief$m="GROUP BY can tell you when each person was last seen. It cannot tell you where, because the group has swallowed the rows that knew. A window function works over a group and leaves every row standing: row_number() OVER (PARTITION BY person_id ORDER BY turn DESC) numbers each person's sightings from the newest back.",task$m="Return the name of everyone whose most recent sighting was in Wexcombe.",why$m="A window function ranks rows inside a group without collapsing the group, which is how you keep the whole row belonging to the latest sighting rather than only its turn number.",world$m="handler",seed$m="academy-window",check$m={kind:"rows",sql:"SELECT p.name FROM people p JOIN (SELECT person_id, city_id, row_number() OVER (PARTITION BY person_id ORDER BY turn DESC) AS nth FROM sightings) s ON s.person_id = p.id WHERE s.nth = 1 AND s.city_id = (SELECT id FROM cities WHERE name = 'Wexcombe')"},hints$m=["Run SELECT person_id, turn, city_id, row_number() OVER (PARTITION BY person_id ORDER BY turn DESC) AS nth FROM sightings and read the nth column.","A window function cannot go in WHERE, because WHERE runs first. Wrap that query in another, or join to it, and filter on nth = 1 there.","SELECT p.name FROM people p JOIN (SELECT person_id, city_id, row_number() OVER (PARTITION BY person_id ORDER BY turn DESC) AS nth FROM sightings) s ON s.person_id = p.id WHERE s.nth = 1 AND s.city_id = (SELECT id FROM cities WHERE name = 'Wexcombe')"],solution$m="SELECT p.name FROM people p JOIN (SELECT person_id, city_id, row_number() OVER (PARTITION BY person_id ORDER BY turn DESC) AS nth FROM sightings) s ON s.person_id = p.id WHERE s.nth = 1 AND s.city_id = (SELECT id FROM cities WHERE name = 'Wexcombe')",__vite_glob_0_40={id:id$m,mode:mode$m,title:title$m,brief:brief$m,task:task$m,why:why$m,world:world$m,seed:seed$m,check:check$m,hints:hints$m,solution:solution$m},id$l="seek-lag",mode$l="seek",title$l="The row before this one",brief$l="Some questions are about two rows at once: this sighting and the one before it. lag() is the window function that reaches back a row, within the partition and in the order you give it. A person's first sighting has nothing before it, so lag gives NULL there, and no comparison with NULL is ever true.",task$l="Return the person_id of everyone who has been seen in the same city on two turns running. Each person once.",why$l="LAG reaches back to the row before this one, in the order you chose. Comparing a row against its neighbour is awkward with joins and ordinary with a window.",world$l="handler",seed$l="academy-lag",check$l={kind:"rows",sql:"SELECT DISTINCT person_id FROM (SELECT person_id, city_id, turn, lag(city_id) OVER trail AS city_before, lag(turn) OVER trail AS turn_before FROM sightings WINDOW trail AS (PARTITION BY person_id ORDER BY turn)) WHERE city_id = city_before AND turn = turn_before + 1"},hints$l=["lag(city_id) OVER (PARTITION BY person_id ORDER BY turn) is the city of that person's previous sighting. lag(turn) over the same window is when it was.","Same city is not enough. Turn 4 and turn 7 in one city is twice, not two turns running: the turns have to be one apart.","SELECT DISTINCT person_id FROM (SELECT person_id, city_id, turn, lag(city_id) OVER trail AS city_before, lag(turn) OVER trail AS turn_before FROM sightings WINDOW trail AS (PARTITION BY person_id ORDER BY turn)) WHERE city_id = city_before AND turn = turn_before + 1"],solution$l="SELECT DISTINCT person_id FROM (SELECT person_id, city_id, turn, lag(city_id) OVER trail AS city_before, lag(turn) OVER trail AS turn_before FROM sightings WINDOW trail AS (PARTITION BY person_id ORDER BY turn)) WHERE city_id = city_before AND turn = turn_before + 1",__vite_glob_0_41={id:id$l,mode:mode$l,title:title$l,brief:brief$l,task:task$l,why:why$l,world:world$l,seed:seed$l,check:check$l,hints:hints$l,solution:solution$l},id$k="seek-recursive",mode$k="seek",title$k="Following the roads",brief$k="One road away is a join. Two is a join on a join. Any number is a query that feeds itself: WITH RECURSIVE starts from some rows, then keeps running its second half on whatever it found last time, until nothing new turns up or you tell it to stop. A map with a loop of roads in it never runs out of new rows, so you always tell it to stop.",task$k="Return the name of every city within three roads of Marrick, Marrick included.",why$k="A recursive CTE follows a chain as far as you tell it to. Roads, parents, replies: anything that points at more of itself is this query with different names.",world$k="handler",seed$k="academy-recursive",check$k={kind:"rows",sql:"WITH RECURSIVE hop(a, b) AS (SELECT city_a, city_b FROM roads UNION SELECT city_b, city_a FROM roads), reach(id, hops) AS (SELECT id, 0 FROM cities WHERE name = 'Marrick' UNION SELECT hop.b, reach.hops + 1 FROM reach JOIN hop ON hop.a = reach.id WHERE reach.hops < 3) SELECT DISTINCT c.name FROM reach JOIN cities c ON c.id = reach.id"},hints$k=["Make the roads run both ways first: hop(a, b) AS (SELECT city_a, city_b FROM roads UNION SELECT city_b, city_a FROM roads).","reach(id, hops) starts at Marrick with 0 hops. Its second half joins reach to hop and adds one, WHERE reach.hops < 3 so that it stops.","WITH RECURSIVE hop(a, b) AS (SELECT city_a, city_b FROM roads UNION SELECT city_b, city_a FROM roads), reach(id, hops) AS (SELECT id, 0 FROM cities WHERE name = 'Marrick' UNION SELECT hop.b, reach.hops + 1 FROM reach JOIN hop ON hop.a = reach.id WHERE reach.hops < 3) SELECT DISTINCT c.name FROM reach JOIN cities c ON c.id = reach.id"],solution$k="WITH RECURSIVE hop(a, b) AS (SELECT city_a, city_b FROM roads UNION SELECT city_b, city_a FROM roads), reach(id, hops) AS (SELECT id, 0 FROM cities WHERE name = 'Marrick' UNION SELECT hop.b, reach.hops + 1 FROM reach JOIN hop ON hop.a = reach.id WHERE reach.hops < 3) SELECT DISTINCT c.name FROM reach JOIN cities c ON c.id = reach.id",__vite_glob_0_42={id:id$k,mode:mode$k,title:title$k,brief:brief$k,task:task$k,why:why$k,world:world$k,seed:seed$k,check:check$k,hints:hints$k,solution:solution$k},id$j="seek-fix-roads",mode$j="seek",title$j="The shortest way",brief$j="A recursive walk finds every way of getting somewhere, not the best one. Out and back again is two roads, so the city you started from turns up at two hops, and so does anywhere that has both a direct road and a roundabout one. A clue that says exactly two roads means the shortest way is two.",task$j="The query in the console is meant to return every city exactly two roads from Marrick by the shortest way. It returns some that are nearer than that. Fix it.",why$j="Reachable within three is not the same as three away. The difference is where you stop counting, and a recursive query will give you the wrong one without complaint.",world$j="handler",seed$j="academy-fix-roads",starter$c=`WITH RECURSIVE
  hop(a, b) AS (SELECT city_a, city_b FROM roads UNION SELECT city_b, city_a FROM roads),
