urn is a transaction",brief$A="BEGIN opens a turn, your orders go inside it, and COMMIT is what makes the world move. Nothing happens until you commit, and an error rolls the whole turn back without charging you for it.",task$A="Open a turn and commit it. You do not have to give an order to end one.",why$A="Nothing has happened until COMMIT. That is the reason a mistake in Command costs nothing: the turn rolls back and you write it again.",world$A="border",seed$A="academy-turn",check$A={kind:"state",sql:"SELECT turn_number > 1 FROM turn_state"},hints$A=["Run BEGIN on its own first.","Then run COMMIT on its own. Watch the turn counter in the top bar.","BEGIN then COMMIT, as two submissions."],solution$A="BEGIN; COMMIT;",__vite_glob_0_26={id:id$A,mode:mode$A,title:title$A,brief:brief$A,task:task$A,why:why$A,world:world$A,seed:seed$A,check:check$A,hints:hints$A,solution:solution$A},id$z="command-move",mode$z="command",title$z="The whole army, one statement",brief$z="An order is an UPDATE, and an UPDATE does not care whether it matches one row or fifty. Moving each unit with its own statement is the slow way to play; moving all of them with one is what the game is teaching you.",task$z="Inside a turn, send every unit you have to city 3, then commit. One UPDATE, not three.",why$z="A turn costs the same whether you spend one statement or five. The WHERE is where the work belongs, and an UPDATE does not care whether it matches one unit or fifty.",world$z="border",seed$z="academy-move",check$z={kind:"state",sql:"SELECT count(*) = (SELECT count(*) FROM units) FROM units WHERE city_id = 3 OR dest_city_id = 3"},hints$z=["dest_city_id is the only column of units an order may set.","WHERE faction_id = 1 catches all of yours at once. You cannot see anybody else's.","BEGIN, then UPDATE units SET dest_city_id = 3 WHERE faction_id = 1, then COMMIT."],solution$z="BEGIN; UPDATE units SET dest_city_id = 3 WHERE faction_id = 1; COMMIT;",__vite_glob_0_27={id:id$z,mode:mode$z,title:title$z,brief:brief$z,task:task$z,why:why$z,world:world$z,seed:seed$z,check:check$z,hints:hints$z,solution:solution$z},id$y="command-build",mode$y="command",title$y="Building without guessing",brief$y="A build order has to carry the right number of turns, and that number is not yours to pick. Rather than look it up and type it, take it from the table in the same statement.",task$y="Inside a turn, queue one infantry in the city you hold, taking the build time from unit_types. Commit when you are done.",why$y="Reading a value beats remembering it. Take the build time from the table and the order stays right when the table changes.",world$y="border",seed$y="academy-build",check$y={kind:"state",sql:"SELECT count(*) > 0 FROM build_queue WHERE faction_id = 1 AND unit_type = 'infantry'"},hints$y=["INSERT INTO build_queue (faction_id, city_id, unit_type, turns_left) takes four values.","Instead of VALUES, use SELECT so you can read build_turns out of unit_types.","INSERT INTO build_queue (faction_id, city_id, unit_type, turns_left) SELECT 1, (SELECT city_id FROM held_city WHERE faction_id = 1), 'infantry', build_turns FROM unit_types WHERE type = 'infantry'"],solution$y="BEGIN; INSERT INTO build_queue (faction_id, city_id, unit_type, turns_left) SELECT 1, (SELECT city_id FROM held_city WHERE faction_id = 1), 'infantry', build_turns FROM unit_types WHERE type = 'infantry'; COMMIT;",__vite_glob_0_28={id:id$y,mode:mode$y,title:title$y,brief:brief$y,task:task$y,why:why$y,world:world$y,seed:seed$y,check:check$y,hints:hints$y,solution:solution$y},id$x="command-view",mode$x="command",title$x="Name a question once",brief$x="A question you will ask every turn is worth naming. A view is a saved query: you write it once and then use its name as if it were a table. Yours last the whole campaign, across every map in it.",task$x="Make a view called my_cities that returns the name and strength of every city you hold.",why$x="A question worth asking twice is worth naming once. Your views last the whole campaign, across every map in it, so what you build up is your own tooling.",world$x="border",seed$x="academy-view",check$x={kind:"state",sql:"SELECT count(*) > 0 FROM sqlite_schema WHERE type = 'view' AND name = 'my_cities'"},hints$x=["CREATE VIEW my_cities AS followed by the query you want to keep.","held_city has city_id and strength; cities has the name.","CREATE VIEW my_cities AS SELECT c.name, h.strength FROM held_city h JOIN cities c ON c.id = h.city_id WHERE h.faction_id = 1"],solution$x="CREATE VIEW my_cities AS SELECT c.name, h.strength FROM held_city h JOIN cities c ON c.id = h.city_id WHERE h.faction_id = 1",__vite_glob_0_29={id:id$x,mode:mode$x,title:title$x,brief:brief$x,task:task$x,why:why$x,world:world$x,seed:seed$x,check:check$x,hints:hints$x,solution:solution$x},id$w="command-pick",mode$w="command",title$w="Orders that pick",brief$w="The WHERE on an UPDATE is the same WHERE you have been writing all along. It decides which rows the order touches, and the rest stay as they were. Scouts are for seeing and infantry is for holding, so you will rarely want to send both to the same place.",task$w="Inside a turn, send your scouts to city 6 and leave everyone else where they are. Commit when you are done.",why$w="An order is defined as much by what it does not match as by what it does. Scouts move, everyone else holds, and that is one statement rather than two.",world$w="border",seed$w="academy-scouts",check$w={kind:"state",sql:"SELECT (SELECT count(*) FROM units WHERE faction_id = 1 AND type = 'scouts' AND (dest_city_id = 6 OR city_id = 6)) = (SELECT count(*) FROM units WHERE faction_id = 1 AND type = 'scouts') AND (SELECT count(*) FROM units WHERE faction_id = 1 AND type <> 'scouts' AND (dest_city_id = 6 OR city_id = 6)) = 0"},hints$w=["SELECT id, type, city_id FROM units shows what you have and what each one is called.","The order is the one from the last lesson with a different WHERE: type = 'scouts'.","BEGIN, then UPDATE units SET dest_city_id = 6 WHERE type = 'scouts', then COMMIT."],solution$w="BEGIN; UPDATE units SET dest_city_id = 6 WHERE type = 'scouts'; COMMIT;",__vite_glob_0_30={id:id$w,mode:mode$w,title:title$w,brief:brief$w,task:task$w,why:why$w,world:world$w,seed:seed$w,check:check$w,hints:hints$w,solution:solution$w},id$v="command-fix-nothing",mode$v="command",title$v="An order nobody heard",brief$v="An UPDATE that matches no rows is not an error. SQLite runs it, changes nothing, and says so only in the count of rows changed, which is printed after every order. A turn committed on the strength of an order like that is a turn wasted.",task$v="A turn is open, and the order in the console is meant to send your scouts to city 13. Run it and read what comes back. Then find out why nobody moved, send them, and commit.",why$v="SQL does not complain about matching nothing. An UPDATE that touches no rows is a perfectly good statement, so the count of rows changed is the only evidence that anything happened.",world$v="border",seed$v="academy-nothing",starter$f="UPDATE units SET dest_city_id = 13 WHERE type = 'scout'",check$v={kind:"state",sql:"SELECT (SELECT count(*) FROM units WHERE faction_id = 1 AND type = 'scouts' AND (dest_city_id = 13 OR city_id = 13)) > 0"},hints$v=["It changed 0 rows. The SET is fine, so the WHERE matched nothing.","SELECT DISTINCT type FROM units shows how the type is spelled.","UPDATE units SET dest_city_id = 13 WHERE type = 'scouts', then COMMIT. The turn is already open."],solution$v="BEGIN; UPDATE units SET dest_city_id = 13 WHERE type = 'scouts'; COMMIT;",__vite_glob_0_31={id:id$v,mode:mode$v,title:title$v,brief:brief$v,task:task$v,why:why$v,world:world$v,seed:seed$v,starter:starter$f,check:check$v,hints:hints$v,solution:solution$v},id$u="command-lookup",mode$u="command",title$u="An order that looks things up",brief$u="The value in a SET does not have to be typed. A query in brackets that returns one value will do, and then the order finds its own target. An order written 