 count(*) FROM units WHERE faction_id = 1 AND type = 'scouts' AND (dest_city_id = 6 OR city_id = 6)) = 0 AND ((SELECT count(*) FROM build_queue WHERE faction_id = 1 AND unit_type = 'scouts') + (SELECT count(*) FROM units WHERE faction_id = 1 AND type = 'scouts')) >= 2"},hints$t=["Two orders inside the same BEGIN and COMMIT: an UPDATE with a WHERE, and an INSERT into build_queue.","turns_left still has to come out of unit_types. The error message says how, if you get it wrong."],solution$t="BEGIN; UPDATE units SET dest_city_id = 6 WHERE type = 'infantry'; INSERT INTO build_queue (faction_id, city_id, unit_type, turns_left) SELECT 1, (SELECT city_id FROM held_city WHERE faction_id = 1), 'scouts', build_turns FROM unit_types WHERE type = 'scouts'; COMMIT;",__vite_glob_0_33={id:id$t,mode:mode$t,title:title$t,brief:brief$t,task:task$t,why:why$t,world:world$t,seed:seed$t,review:review$4,check:check$t,hints:hints$t,solution:solution$t},id$s="command-objective",mode$s="command",title$s="What the map is fought over",brief$s="Every battle is settled by an objective, and the objective is a row in a table like everything else. On the first map it is cities. On this one it is a single region, and only the cities inside it count. Most of the map is road you cross to get there, so the first query of the battle is the one that tells you where there is.",task$s="Return the name of every city in the region this battle is fought over. Take the region from the objective table, not from your own reading of it.",why$s="What a battle is fought over is a row you can read rather than a rule you have to be told. The bots read the same row.",world$s="crossroads",seed$s="academy-objective",check$s={kind:"rows",sql:"SELECT name FROM cities WHERE region_id = (SELECT region_id FROM objective)"},hints$s=["SELECT * FROM objective shows one row: what kind of battle this is, and which region_id counts.","cities has a region_id too. The objective's goes in brackets where you would have typed the number.","SELECT name FROM cities WHERE region_id = (SELECT region_id FROM objective)"],solution$s="SELECT name FROM cities WHERE region_id = (SELECT region_id FROM objective)",__vite_glob_0_34={id:id$s,mode:mode$s,title:title$s,brief:brief$s,task:task$s,why:why$s,world:world$s,seed:seed$s,check:check$s,hints:hints$s,solution:solution$s},id$r="seek-null",mode$r="seek",title$r="Nothing is not a value",brief$r="Ghost opens the whole schema, and units.dest_city_id is the first column in it that can be empty. Empty is NULL, and NULL means unknown. Is an unknown equal to an unknown? SQL says that is unknown too, so = NULL is never true for any row. It is not an error either, which is the trouble.",task$r="The query in the console is meant to return the id of every unit with no destination. It returns nothing, and there are plenty. Fix it.",why$r="NULL is not a value, it is the absence of one, so = never matches it and neither does <>. IS NULL is the only thing that asks the question.",world$r="ghost",seed$r="academy-null",starter$e="SELECT id FROM units WHERE dest_city_id = NULL",check$r={kind:"rows",sql:"SELECT id FROM units WHERE dest_city_id IS NULL"},hints$r=["SELECT count(*), count(dest_city_id) FROM units shows the gap. count(column) skips NULLs and count(*) does not.","The test for empty is IS NULL. Its opposite is IS NOT NULL.","SELECT id FROM units WHERE dest_city_id IS NULL"],solution$r="SELECT id FROM units WHERE dest_city_id IS NULL",__vite_glob_0_35={id:id$r,mode:mode$r,title:title$r,brief:brief$r,task:task$r,why:why$r,world:world$r,seed:seed$r,starter:starter$e,check:check$r,hints:hints$r,solution:solution$r},id$q="seek-left-join",mode$q="seek",title$q="Keeping the rows with no match",brief$q="A plain JOIN drops any row with nothing to pair with, so it can never show you what is missing. LEFT JOIN keeps every row of the table on the left, and where nothing on the right matched it fills the right-hand columns with NULL. Filter for that NULL and you have the rows with no match.",task$q="Return the name of every city that has no unit stationed in it.",why$q="A LEFT JOIN keeps the rows with nothing on the other side, and the columns from that side come back NULL. Filtering on that NULL is how you ask for absence with a join.",world$q="ghost",seed$q="academy-left-join",check$q={kind:"rows",sql:"SELECT c.name FROM cities c LEFT JOIN units u ON u.city_id = c.id WHERE u.id IS NULL"},hints$q=["Start with SELECT c.name, u.id FROM cities c LEFT JOIN units u ON u.city_id = c.id and look for the empty cells.","u.id is never NULL in a real unit, so where it is NULL there was no unit to join.","SELECT c.name FROM cities c LEFT JOIN units u ON u.city_id = c.id WHERE u.id IS NULL"],solution$q="SELECT c.name FROM cities c LEFT JOIN units u ON u.city_id = c.id WHERE u.id IS NULL",__vite_glob_0_36={id:id$q,mode:mode$q,title:title$q,brief:brief$q,task:task$q,why:why$q,world:world$q,seed:seed$q,check:check$q,hints:hints$q,solution:solution$q},id$p="seek-not-in-null",mode$p="seek",title$p="The list with a hole in it",brief$p="NOT IN asks whether a value differs from every item in the list. Against NULL that question has no answer, so the whole test comes back unknown and the row is dropped.",task$p="Return the name of every city no unit is heading to. The query in the console is the obvious way to write it, and it returns nothing at all.",why$p="One NULL anywhere in the list empties the result, and nothing about the query looks wrong. This is why NOT EXISTS is the safer habit: it asks whether a matching row exists, which is a question NULL cannot refuse to answer.",world$p="ghost",seed$p="academy-not-in-null",setup$7="UPDATE units SET dest_city_id = 4 WHERE id IN (1, 3); UPDATE units SET dest_city_id = 9 WHERE id IN (5, 8, 11); UPDATE units SET dest_city_id = 14 WHERE id = 2",starter$d="SELECT name FROM cities WHERE id NOT IN (SELECT dest_city_id FROM units)",check$p={kind:"rows",sql:"SELECT name FROM cities WHERE id NOT IN (SELECT dest_city_id 